{'body': "Hey Sam,

I hope you're doing well. I was going through our code again after our meeting, and I noticed a bug related to time zone handling. When a user selects a different time zone, the calendar doesn't update correctly. I think I know how to fix it, but I wanted to run it by you first.

The issue is in the `updateCalendar` method. We need to use the `TimeZone` class to get the correct time zone offset and apply it to the date. Here's a code snippet that should resolve the issue:

```java
private void updateCalendar(String timeZoneId) {
    TimeZone timeZone = TimeZone.getTimeZone(timeZoneId);
    int offset = timeZone.getOffset(new Date().getTime());

    Calendar calendar = Calendar.getInstance();
    calendar.setTimeZone(timeZone);
    calendar.setTimeInMillis(calendar.getTimeInMillis() + offset);

    // Update the calendar display
    // ...
}
```

Let me know if this makes sense, or if you have any questions. I'm happy to explain further if needed.

Best,
Alex", 'from': 'Alex Johnson <ajohnson@university.edu>', 'subject': 'Time zone bug in our project', 'to': 'Sam Lee <slee@university.edu>'}

